title: v6.1 Sprint 1 Wave 3 shipped — paradigm override CLI live
summary: 'Wave 3 added `paradigm override` public command with three subcommands (clear-by-id, list, clear-all --force). Builder used direct-import pattern (overrideList calls getActiveRemediations() in-process from internal/active-remediations.ts) — no subprocess overhead. Refactored Wave 2''s helper to extract pure getActiveRemediations() function; existing activeRemediationsCommand wraps it. Hook output bytes unchanged. CLI-side overrides.jsonl format matches bash-side exactly (only differs on mechanism: cli vs env). Three advisor-flagged fixes: double-checkmark stripped from output, explicit process.exit(1) after errors, atomic fs.rename for archive. 320 paradigm tests / 1 skip + 233 paradigm-mcp tests (no regression).'
symbols_touched:
  - '#paradigm-override-cli'
  - '#active-remediations'
  - '#paradigm-propose-block'
symbols_created:
  - '#paradigm-override-cli'
files_created:
  - packages/paradigm/src/commands/override.ts
files_modified:
  - packages/paradigm/src/commands/internal/active-remediations.ts
  - packages/paradigm/src/index.ts
  - packages/paradigm/src/commands/.purpose
learnings:
  - Direct in-process import beats subprocess for CLI tools that consume internal helpers — single source of truth, faster, no error-surface duplication. Pattern applies to all future Stop hook helper / CLI command pairs.
  - 'Atomic fs.rename for archive moves matters: a copy+unlink sequence has a window where the file exists in both locations or neither. fs.rename is atomic at the filesystem level. Important for state files like remediations.'
  - cli-output.ts success() helper already prepends ✓ — don't double it. Read the helper before adding decoration.
  - error() helper writes to stderr but does NOT terminate — explicit process.exit(1) needed after each error() call when the intent is to fail.
